  <section class="lp-section lp-profile" style="background:#E8E4DC;border-top:1px solid #D8D2C8;">
    <div class="lp-section-inner">
      <div class="lp-section-label">Elmhurst Architecture &amp; Masonry Profile</div>
      <h2 class="lp-section-h2">What Makes Elmhurst Masonry <em>Different</em></h2>
      <div class="lp-profile-grid">
        <div class="lp-profile-card">
          <div class="lp-profile-card-label">Primary Building Era</div>
          <div class="lp-profile-card-value">1910s – 1960s</div>
          <p class="lp-profile-card-desc">Prairie-style homes from the 1910s–1920s need lime putty; mid-century colonials from the 1940s–1960s need Type S or N Portland blends. Getting the era wrong in DuPage County means getting the mortar wrong — and that causes irreversible spalling.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-profile-card">
          <div class="lp-profile-card-label">Dominant Brick Type</div>
          <div class="lp-profile-card-value">Chicago Common, Clay &amp; Buff Modular</div>
          <p class="lp-profile-card-desc">Elmhurst spans Chicago Common in early buildings to buff and red clay modular in mid-century construction. Each brick type has different absorption rates and mortar compatibility — we identify which is present before prescribing any repair.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-profile-card">
          <div class="lp-profile-card-label">Climate Risk</div>
          <div class="lp-profile-card-value">High — 90+ Freeze-Thaw Cycles/Year</div>
          <p class="lp-profile-card-desc">Chicagoland's 90+ annual freeze-thaw cycles force moisture into failing mortar joints — accelerating crumbling, spalling, and water infiltration behind the brick face every winter.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- COMMON MASONRY PROBLEMS -->
  <section class="lp-section lp-problems" style="background:#F5F3EF;border-top:1px solid #D8D2C8;">
    <div class="lp-section-inner">
      <div class="lp-section-label">Common Masonry Problems in Elmhurst</div>
      <h2 class="lp-section-h2">What We Fix — <em>Most Often in Elmhurst</em></h2>
      <div class="lp-problems-grid">
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Prairie-Era Lime Mortar Failure</h3>
          <p>After 100+ years, lime putty mortar on Elmhurst's Prairie-style homes has shrunk and crumbled in exposed joints. Repair requires lime-based mortar — Portland cement on these homes destroys irreplaceable historic brick face.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Mid-Century Chimney Joint Cracking</h3>
          <p>1940s–1960s Elmhurst colonials show consistent chimney crown and upper-course cracking. Crown failure lets water in — causing interior damage and eventually requiring full chimney rebuilding.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Brick Spalling from Incorrect Previous Repairs</h3>
          <p>Portland cement over soft Chicago Common or lime-set brick traps moisture and forces the brick face to delaminate. Correcting prior incompatible repairs is a major part of our Elmhurst work in DuPage County.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Grade-Level Mortar Deterioration on North Elevations</h3>
          <p>North-facing grade-level brick experiences the most aggressive freeze-thaw cycling and fails first — often below sightline from the street, unnoticed until damage is extensive.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

